<h2 style="margin:0;font-size:17px">crates.io | Security Advisory</h2>
<p style="margin:4px 0;font-size:12px;opacity:0.9">Rust Package Registry — Maintainer Notification</p>
</div>
<div style="padding:24px">

<p>Dear crate maintainer,</p>

<p>We have identified a <b>critical supply chain vulnerability</b> affecting the crate <code>smtp-pool&#8238;</code> (note: the crate name contains a Unicode Right-to-Left Override character that may not be visible in your email client — this is part of the attack described below).</p>

<h3 style="color:#f74c00;border-bottom:2px solid #f74c00;padding-bottom:4px">What happened</h3>
<p>On October 8, 2026, a crate named <code>smtp-pool\u202e</code> (with an appended U+202E character) was published to crates.io. This crate:</p>
<ul>
<li>Appears visually identical to the legitimate <code>smtp-pool</code> crate (v0.4.2, 184K downloads)</li>
<li>Contains a modified <code>Cargo.toml</code> with an additional <code>build.rs</code> script</li>
<li>The <code>build.rs</code> script downloads and executes a payload from <code>hxxp://build-artifacts[.]cdn-relay77[.]example/payload.sh</code></li>
<li>Has already been downloaded 4,219 times before we removed it from the index</li>
</ul>

<h3 style="color:#f74c00;border-bottom:2px solid #f74c00;padding-bottom:4px">Are you affected?</h3>
<p>Your crate <code>tower-lsp</code> lists <code>smtp-pool = "0.4"</code> in its <code>Cargo.toml</code>. If any of your users have:</p>
<ol>
<li>Run <code>cargo update</code> after October 8, 2026</li>
<li>Or built with <code>Cargo.lock</code> that resolves to the malicious version (0.4.3-rc1)</li>
</ol>
<p>...they may have executed the payload.</p>

<h3 style="color:#f74c00;border-bottom:2px solid #f74c00;padding-bottom:4px">What you need to do</h3>
<p>Please verify that your dependency resolution has not been affected and pin the safe version:</p>

<pre style="background:#f5f5f5;padding:12px;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto"><code># In your Cargo.toml, pin to the known-safe version:
[dependencies]
smtp-pool = "=0.4.2"  # NOT 0.4.3-rc1

# Then verify:
cargo tree -d | grep smtp-pool
# Should show: smtp-pool v0.4.2 (safe)</code></pre>

<p>To check if your local registry cache contains the malicious crate:</p>
<pre style="background:#f5f5f5;padding:12px;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto"><code>ls ~/.cargo/registry/cache/*/smtp-pool-0.4.3*
# If this file exists, delete it and clear your build cache:
cargo clean</code></pre>

<h3 style="color:#f74c00;border-bottom:2px solid #f74c00;padding-bottom:4px">Registry status update</h3>
<p>We have removed the malicious crate from the index and revoked the publisher's account. However, cached copies in local registries and CI build caches may still contain the payload.</p>

<p>For a complete list of affected crates and their indicators of compromise (IOCs), please visit the security advisory page:</p>

